From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Ray)
Date: Mon, 27 Jun 94 4:14:12 WET DST
Subject: [#94-6-161] Scenarios: What happens when nanotechnology appears?

  In the two nation scenario, each with the capability of destroying
the other, the MAD equilibium should prevent the scenario from being
carried out. However, we must proceed with the assumption that
proliferation will occur and that sooner or later, one nation, 
organization, or nutcase, will release a nano-strike. (I'm confident
of this possibility, just look at all the nutcases opening fire 
into crowds. It seems almost everyweek, I see on the news, a story
of someone who walks into their place of employment and guns down several
coworkers)

  What arguments can be made against your scenario? Well, for one thing,
I don't buy the simplistic exponential replicator gobbling up the
earth design. Any replicator which attempted an exponential growth
would quickly burn itself out as resources wouldn't be able to be
aquired fast enough. Next, the replicator would need to be able to
efficiently survive in any climate without the ecosystem either
outcompetiting it for resources (thus starving itself), or gobbling
up the replicator itself. As I see it, the speed of replication would
have to be finely tuned so the replicator wouldn't use up resources
quickly in areas where resources are not as plentiful (otherwise it would
die out)

  Far better than trying to starve biological life to death by
depleting resources, a better graygoo would simply target human
biology. Simply attack the liver or some other vital organ (does
AIDS ring a bell?) 

  Anyway, no matter what the mechanism, I don't see graygoo spreading
around the globe from a single release point in hours, or days. I'd
say it would take years or decades to complete its task, more than
enough time to figure a way to stop it, or get off the planet.
(of course it could do it faster, but I'm being conservative and assuming
that the replicator would not spread at its maximal rate so as to be
ale to survive in many resource environments)

  And with the expense of developing nanotech, why should we worry at
all at this point when nuclear weapons can do the job quicker and
easier. Rapid nuclear proliferation will most likely occur before the
first superpowers get phase one nanotech. Furthermore, it is far easier
to modify existing bacteria or viruses to spread a plague, no need to
reinvent the wheel.

   By the time we get nanotech capable of doing what you envision, we might
be heavily entrenched in space.

   If replicators are so good at outcompetiting macroscopic life, why
don't bacteria rule the earth? Must be something inefficient about
small simple lifeforms.

-Ray


