Subject: [#94-6-165] Request for Comments: U.S. Tech Corps

On Sun, 26 Jun 1994, Ray wrote:

> 
>    I'd expect that if the public school system were eliminated, there would
> be quite a massive industry in education loans. Some of the really big

 And later on, a *lot* of loan defaults, too.

>     I attended a Catholic middle school for a few years and it provided
> a great education (why did I attend if I'm an atheist? Because the
> neighborhood public schools had an epidemic of crime. Metal detectors
> were installed at the entrances, that's how bad it was.) My tuition 
> was only $70/month. My mother was able to afford to pay this while
> only making $7/hour.

My experience moving to a private school was similar (weapons in public
school and all) except the tuition was much higher.  I paid for it
myself, though, by working p/t.

> 
>    Why not have a totally private school system with government 
> subsidy of those who can't afford just like college? The government
> could back loans up for those with bad credit too.

But what if the only private school in the area doesn't want them (for
race, religion, sex reasons) - private schools do tend to have an "old
boy" system when it comes to accepting students.

In Hawaii, up until a few months ago, our Insurance companies were required
to insure any driver who is willing to pay their rates.  But it just didn't
work.  I'd be suspect of similar mandates for private schools.  

>    Has it occured to you that perhaps the problem isn't structural
> unemployment? Sure, you can train hicks for jobs at Boeing, but what if
> there aren't any jobs at Boeing?

Agreed that if you are in a town built around a particular industry, and
that industry is laying off left and right, training for local jobs would
be futile.  But there are certain basic skills that can help someone prepare
for jobs which are common to most all industries.  Most all businesses
need at least one person to do office support.  Many need someone who has
training or skills in basic bookkeeping, word processing, telephones,
etc.  Most towns need persons trained in the service industry (food service,
hotel support staff, etc.)  

If we can at least help make it possible for them to pursue their GED it
is a step in the right direction.  If we help make it possible for them to
get an A.A or A.S. it can make them more competitive.

But at least projects like U.S. Tech are trying to do something about the
problem.  If we don't like the current approach, then instead of being
vocal against it, we should start an alternative within our community.

If the private schools and business are to subsidize education for socio-
economically impaired students, there will still be a need for someone
to act as catalyst and to follow through.

I would like to see AFDC pooled in such a manner where say 30 mothers
act as a "cell" with each running a day care center one day a month.
The remainder of the days they could go to school, go to work, hunt for
jobs.  If they still can't find work, they could be put to work part time
for the government/program office until such time they can find work.  At
least this way we would get return on investment.

Just a few thoughts.


-NetSurfer

