From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Mon, 27 Jun 1994 12:51:16 -0700
Subject: [#94-6-169] Scenarios: What happens when nanotechnology appears?

My comments will largely echo Ray's.

Gregory Sullivan writes:
>Consider the following simplified situation. Life has not yet moved off of
>the planet and the world population has aligned itself into multiple warring
>factions. Two of the factions each release gray-goo-like weapons
>specifically targeted at all the opposition parties.  Each of the two
>parties can effectively defend itself against its own carefully designed
>gray goo; however, each of the two parties is unable to defend itself
>against the gray goo of its adversary. All the other parties succumb to one
>or both of the gray-goo-like weapons. More realistically, perhaps, a
>sequence of weapons of varying capabilities will be designed and released by
>many parties. It is possible that all parties - even non-participants - will
>fall prey to one or more of the weapons.

Greg asks us to worry about nanotech, but it seems to me nanotech is
pretty irrelevant to his concerns.  He is instead worried about "total
war".  If today's world were to be split into two unified and roughly
equally-capable factions hell-bent on wiping the other off the map, we
would likely face scenarios as serious as any Greg has described.

Nanotech does offer new possibilities for the technology of war, but I
don't yet see it really changing that much about the possibility and
consequences of such total war.  The only remotely plausible argument
I've heard is the one Drexler presented here, that a *transition* to
nanotech would suddenly place overwhelming force in the hands of a
single military power, which would then "take over".  (I don't yet buy
this argument, however.)

So instead thinking about replicators and atomic bending angles, I
suggest Greg think more about plain old fashion war.  What causes war?
What causes large vs. small alliances?  What causes total vs. limited
war?  Until you have a handle on these issues, I don't think you're
going to make much progress thinking about nanotech war.

Robin Hanson

